WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.892
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:07.020
ANKUR KOTWAL: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.270
My name is Ankur Kotwal.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:09.230
And I'm joined--

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.850
LAURENCE MORONEY: I'm Laurence.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.350
ANKUR KOTWAL: --by
Laurence Moroney.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.680
We're developer advocates
on the Google Maps API team.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.780
And today, we're
going to talk to you

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.760
about location intelligence.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.390
And if you've been attending
many of the Google Maps API

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.960
sessions at Cloud Next, or
you've been following along

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.220
on YouTube, you've probably
heard this term a few times.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.040
I'm going to define it for the
sake of the folks that are new.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.980
But location
intelligence is basically

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.100
the idea of taking all
of this location data

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.830
and adding context.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:44.580
Adding that real-time
nature so that you

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.330
can infer useful
information out of our APIs

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:53.550
and surface that to your
users in interesting ways.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.700
And in this session, we're
going to really synthesize

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.370
that entire narrative
of location intelligence

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.500
that we've been talking
about at Cloud Next

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.415
and show you how you can walk
up and down a stack of location.

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.070
So first I'm going to start
off with a very brief history

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.250
lesson, and that's the fact
that it wasn't that long

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.550
ago-- just over a decade ago--
that we were using paper maps,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.270
right?

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.660
It seems like a very
archaic concept today,

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:26.350
but it wasn't that long ago that
we had this experience, where

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.080
if you had to find
your current location,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.120
you'd have to know
the address, and you'd

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.040
have to look up this index
of really tiny fonts,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.595
and then find a particular
location, find it on a map.

00:01:40.595 --> 00:01:42.095
LAURENCE MORONEY:
Anybody got a pen?

00:01:42.095 --> 00:01:43.820
ANKUR KOTWAL: [LAUGHS]
And then, once you

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.360
wanted to go somewhere, you
had to do the same process

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.657
on where to go.

00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:50.240
And then the hard
part really started,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.060
which is you had to
navigate your way there.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:57.080
I haven't navigated my way to
anything for many, many years

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.270
now, and that's because
my user behavior,

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.830
my behavior has changed.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.930
And I'm sure that's the
same for many of you.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.670
When we moved into
the digital world,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.500
there were a number
of companies that

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.680
had that audacious goal
of mapping the world.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.960
And of course, Google
was one of those.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.350
But as our smart phones
got richer and richer,

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.016
again, as our user
behaviors changed--

00:02:20.016 --> 00:02:22.390
I'm going to give you a simple
example of a user behavior

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:23.890
that changed.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.820
When you were using those maps,
and you needed to move across

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.355
to the next edge
of that page, you

00:02:30.355 --> 00:02:33.250
used to actually have to
find the page to jump to.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.964
You remember the
map books, right?

00:02:35.964 --> 00:02:38.380
You think about our experience
today, we just swipe left--

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:39.910
LAURENCE MORONEY: I'm going to
stay in one of the city, right?

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:40.660
ANKUR KOTWAL: Yes.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.700
You just now swipe
left or right,

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.580
and we have that seamless
connected experience.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.740
That's an example of
a behavior of ours,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:49.930
a fundamental
behavior that changed,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.120
and our expectations shifted.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.150
And so as we started to
have smart phones that

00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:00.280
were well connected, we
started to demand seeing more

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.590
of the dynamic world, right?

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.750
Having that
information come to us.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.850
So I gave you an
example about navigation

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.190
and how hard it was already
to navigate from point A

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:15.170
to point B. That was at least
a static route, a static route.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.110
My accent kicks in
between American

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.901
and Australian
between now and then.

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:21.400
LAURENCE MORONEY: I will try
to be the Australian to English

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.301
translator, but do bear in mind
that I'm Irish, so kind of--

00:03:25.301 --> 00:03:26.550
ANKUR KOTWAL: Translator fail.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:26.860
LAURENCE MORONEY: Yeah.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:27.170
I might be.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.900
So there might be an
impedance mismatch.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.149
ANKUR KOTWAL: But again,
looking at user expectations,

00:03:31.149 --> 00:03:33.760
we expect our mapping
apps, whether it's

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.220
Google Maps or any
other app, to tell us

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.120
when we're going to be
impacted in our journey, right?

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.520
When we have a
flight delay, when

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.190
there's an accident on the
road or some roadworks,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.030
whatever it may be.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.780
And the way we're trying
to overcome this problem

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.720
is by having more
and more sensors,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.740
more connected devices.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.340
And we're starting to see
that with IoT devices, right?

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.964
But as we gather
all of this data,

00:03:59.964 --> 00:04:01.380
just because you
have data doesn't

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.360
mean you have intelligence.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.670
And it's really
machine learning that's

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.540
able to process this data
at scale in real time

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.650
or near real time, and make it
predictive to add that value,

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.106
to add that intelligence.

00:04:15.106 --> 00:04:16.980
And that's what I'm
talking about when I talk

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.950
about location intelligence.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:26.420
Now, let's switch from this
to something more about today.

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:29.810
If you think about how we
think about our location,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.850
we think about our location
with maps, maps in our pockets.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.650
We have the whole world
in our pockets, now.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.170
We think of it as maybe
a latitude and longitude

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.356
because, depending on the
application you're using,

00:04:40.356 --> 00:04:41.230
that's what you need.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:45.910
You might think of your
location as an address,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.940
or you might think of
your location as a place.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:53.790
Today, we're on floor three
of the Moscone Center.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.030
Can I get a show of hands?

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.830
How many of you know the
address of the Moscone Center?

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.730
There is not a single
hand up, just for the--

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.050
oh, one over there.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:04.560
Excellent.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.018
LAURENCE MORONEY: How many know
the latitude and longitude?

00:05:07.018 --> 00:05:08.406
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.400
ANKUR KOTWAL: My point
is, as people, we

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.930
think of our location in names
that are more familiar to us.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.390
You might know the
address of your house--

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.200
I hope you do--

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.830
and you obviously probably know
the address of your workplace.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.070
But generally, other sort of
temporary places we visit,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.500
we don't think of our
locations in addresses

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.170
or latitude and longitude.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:34.950
But those latitudes, longitudes,
addresses, and places,

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:39.180
they all have their place when
we use them in an API context.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:43.350
And navigating across those,
serving our users' needs

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:48.670
across each of those, is really
important and not always easy.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.070
Laurence and I are here
today to show you how you

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.590
can do this in an easier way.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:57.530
I'm going to first also
take a slight diversion

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.780
and talk about an example
that you've probably, again,

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.070
heard about a few times now in
some of these sessions, which

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.580
is ride sharing.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.580
And the reason I want to
talk about ride sharing

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.640
is I'm going to go into the
deep technical aspects of it.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.800
If you think about how
we do ride sharing today,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.290
we don't wait on a
sidewalk waiting for a cab

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:16.550
to come for us.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.850
I mean, sometimes we do, but
if you're using the apps,

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:21.590
the experience is
very different.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:22.430
We pull out the app.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.201
The app automatically
knows our location.

00:06:25.201 --> 00:06:25.700
Right?

00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:28.820
So it can work out where we need
to be picked up very quickly.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.030
When we choose our destination,
we're not swiping our map

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.920
or panning and zooming
all over the place.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.170
We're doing a search
for our destination.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.140
We type in a few
characters, and suddenly it

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.180
knows-- you want
the SFO Airport,

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.250
or the Intercontinental Hotel.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:45.360
Whatever the place may be.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.600
And then when you say, hey,
I want to request a cab,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:54.420
it automatically finds a driver
for you that's closest to you,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:57.420
and gives you a very
accurate estimate of how long

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.770
it's going to take for
that driver to get to you.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:01.770
Again, we're looking
at changes in behavior,

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.870
but think about the
underlying technology

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.220
that goes behind this.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.130
Your current location
might be GPS,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.500
or you might be
indoors at the time

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:12.810
that you started
looking for this.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.390
So we have to use some sort
of indoor mapping technology

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:18.610
to find our locations, because
GPS needs line of sight,

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.537
and when you're indoors, you
don't have that line of sight.

00:07:21.537 --> 00:07:23.120
When we're looking
at our destination,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.270
we have to use a database
that knows about all

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:27.950
of these places in the world.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.260
I'm going to talk about
that database in a minute.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.660
And then of course, there's
the dynamic routing.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:37.460
As that taxi driver or cab
driver is being sent to you,

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.520
it's able to route them in
the quickest possible way

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.850
to avoid traffic, to avoid
roadworks, and give you

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:48.286
that estimate, that
very accurate estimate.

00:07:48.286 --> 00:07:50.060
So it's really,
really important to be

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.360
able to walk across this
stack of understanding

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.640
the driver's location and the
user, the passenger's location.

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:03.110
So we're really on the cusp of
this revolution around location

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.190
intelligence.

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:07.470
And what it's enabling
us to do is power many,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.940
many use cases in how you
can bring our Google Maps

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.610
APIs together.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.810
Location intelligence
is revolutionary.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.950
We just talked about ride
sharing as an example.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.927
A year ago, we
would not have had--

00:08:22.927 --> 00:08:24.760
actually, I'm going to
talk about industries

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.400
in a second, which
is right here.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.380
A year ago, when we
looked at the industries

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:31.450
that our use cases
were useful, we

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:33.460
wouldn't have included
gaming, right?

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:37.360
Because whilst we had some
location or real-world games,

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:40.030
it hadn't exploded
in as big a way.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:45.170
There was a talk earlier
today about real world gaming

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:49.760
and how "Pokemon Go" has
really changed the field here.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.490
In the last few months,
since "Pokemon Go" came out,

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.710
there have been hundreds
of millions of players

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:57.850
that have gotten involved.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:00.910
And it's because we
are now able to provide

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:04.480
this kind of location
intelligence to our users.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.330
It's not just about
putting a pin on a map,

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:09.880
it's about knowing
where they can

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.123
go, where there are
other players about,

00:09:13.123 --> 00:09:14.831
whether the stores
that they are going to

00:09:14.831 --> 00:09:17.680
are open right now or not, and
using that in meaningful ways.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.930
LAURENCE MORONEY: And it was
really interesting in how

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.530
it was able to bring
innovation to an industry that

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.330
wasn't innovating in that way.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.750
Gaming was all about the next
console, the more powerful,

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.510
adding more and more power, and
innovation was going in that.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.760
But then suddenly, when
somebody had the realization

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.670
to bring geo awareness and
location awareness into it,

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.607
we had a whole new
genre of gaming open up.

00:09:39.607 --> 00:09:41.190
ANKUR KOTWAL: And
so what we're seeing

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.700
is when we use location
intelligence, when

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.270
we use maps APIs, we're
affecting existing industries

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:50.820
and helping to optimize
those user experiences,

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:53.910
but we're also creating
new ones as time goes on

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.690
or disrupting ones that you
wouldn't have traditionally

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:58.735
thought were relevant.

00:09:58.735 --> 00:10:00.360
LAURENCE MORONEY: I
have a favorite one

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.094
in the services on demand
space that I just learned

00:10:03.094 --> 00:10:04.260
about a couple of weeks ago.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.110
That my daughter is in
university, and being

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.270
a student, she
doesn't cook a lot,

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.160
so she gets a lot
of takeout food.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.230
But typically, if you
order delivery food,

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.720
you have to have a minimum
order, or they won't deliver.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.870
And typically, she's
a five foot girl,

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.330
and she doesn't eat a lot, and
she can't spend enough money

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.800
to have them deliver it.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.460
So in the university
campus that she's at,

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:27.990
there is this delivery
service that what they do

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:30.120
is that they aggregate
everything together.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.280
So she and four
other girls could

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.370
order from five
different restaurants.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.030
And then these
people will do that

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.430
and go to the five
different restaurants,

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.794
get the food for them,
and deliver it to them.

00:10:40.794 --> 00:10:42.210
And then the
restaurant is winning

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.751
because they're getting delivery
customers that they normally

00:10:44.751 --> 00:10:46.952
wouldn't get because
they had a price barrier.

00:10:46.952 --> 00:10:49.410
The kids are winning, so they're
not spending as much money

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.690
as they normally would have
had to get something delivered.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:53.148
And then this
service that came in,

00:10:53.148 --> 00:10:55.650
that's able to geolocate
where the kids are,

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:58.560
and then able to use geolocation
to find the restaurants.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.060
And when the kid
does an order, they

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.010
can check against the
menu for that restaurant

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.840
to see that they are
not being ripped off,

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:04.800
and stuff like that.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.461
And then this whole new service
has grown up almost overnight.

00:11:07.461 --> 00:11:08.460
And everybody's winning.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.080
It's really cool.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.640
ANKUR KOTWAL: So
in this session,

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:16.289
we're going to talk about three
ways that we see location.

00:11:16.289 --> 00:11:18.580
We're going to talk about
latitude and longitude, which

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.204
is traditionally
delivered through GPS.

00:11:20.204 --> 00:11:21.940
We'll talk about
addresses, and we're

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:23.065
going to talk about places.

00:11:23.065 --> 00:11:25.784
So I'm going to hand over to
Laurence to take you through.

00:11:25.784 --> 00:11:26.950
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thank you.

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.630
So latitude and longitude.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.970
When we think about the
world that we live in,

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:33.340
every point on the
surface of this sphere

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:35.444
called planet Earth
has a location.

00:11:35.444 --> 00:11:36.860
There's a number
of different ways

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.980
that you can measure where
a point is on a sphere,

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:42.650
but the most common in our
world is latitude and longitude.

00:11:42.650 --> 00:11:44.650
And so when you're doing
latitude and longitude,

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:46.524
then you can know exactly
where that point is

00:11:46.524 --> 00:11:48.820
on the surface of the
sphere, and there's a system

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.040
called GPS in order to do that.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.340
Most of us have phones with
a built-in GPS on them,

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:56.110
and being able to
access that GPS,

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:58.840
being able to program against
that GPS, is pretty easy.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:00.470
It's pretty straightforward.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.500
So if you're using
Android as an example,

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.340
Android has a thing called
a Fused Location Provider.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.090
And what the Fused
Location Provider does

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.120
is it brings in a number of
signals from different places--

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:14.429
from GPS, from network signals,
from sniffing networks.

00:12:14.429 --> 00:12:15.970
If you were at my
session earlier on,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:18.190
you saw I had a little
robot that was doing that.

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:21.261
From beacons, cell towers, geo
fences, lots of other things

00:12:21.261 --> 00:12:21.760
like that.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.700
Lots of other sensors that
it fuses all of this together

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.520
to give you the best estimate
of where you are, and GPS

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:29.140
is one of the signals into that.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.810
If you're an iOS developer,
the core location APIs

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.190
do the same thing.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:35.350
They allow you to access
the GPS on the phone

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:37.750
so that you can get a
sense of where you are,

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:39.430
latitude and longitude wise.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:42.550
But as Ankur mentioned, you
don't always see the GPS.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:43.990
You might be
indoors, or you might

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:46.281
live in a big city like New
York City, where the canyon

00:12:46.281 --> 00:12:48.010
effect of the
skyscrapers can block

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.560
your access to the satellite
so that you can't get

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:52.160
a good read on where you are.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.280
So other signals, such
as beacons and Wi-Fi

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:55.999
and all that kind of
thing are available.

00:12:55.999 --> 00:12:57.790
So you can get your
latitude and longitude,

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:00.216
and you can see as
accurately as possible

00:13:00.216 --> 00:13:02.590
where you are on the surface
of the planet at this moment

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:03.580
in time.

00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:06.850
Now, for programming, on
Android it's very, very simple

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.530
on the Fused Location Provider.

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:11.140
It fires an event called
onLocationChanged.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:14.080
And onLocationChanged has
an object called location.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:14.960
And guess what?

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.400
Two the properties of this
are latitude and longitude

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.900
that you can get by calling the
getLatitude and getLongitude

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:20.710
methods.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:22.859
So pretty easy if you're
an Android developer

00:13:22.859 --> 00:13:25.150
to be able to get the lat-long
of your current location

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:26.950
or of your device's
current location.

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.160
Similar as an iOS developer,
the CL Location Manager,

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.410
which is part of core
location, gives you back,

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:34.570
and you can set it
for desired accuracy,

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:36.541
for example, the nearest
10 meters, I think.

00:13:36.541 --> 00:13:37.790
That's pretty accurate, right?

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:40.960
And then what you do is
you can start monitoring it

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.540
for significant changes,
which I really like how

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:45.130
they've done this in the API.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:48.400
So what that means is that
if I've set it for 10 meter,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.980
then a significant change
is something of 10 meters

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:53.320
or above, and then it will
fire an event back to me.

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:56.620
Because GPS can be very
expensive battery-wise,

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:59.110
heat-wise, for you to
actually use on your phone,

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:00.910
so something like those
significant changes

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:02.230
will fire that event.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.420
And then out of the
location manager,

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:06.370
you can read the
latitude and longitude.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.680
So again, you can
get the point where

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.425
you are on this planet
with reasonable accuracy.

00:14:11.425 --> 00:14:14.440
ANKUR KOTWAL: Laurence, if I
had a internet of things device,

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:17.677
and I was to stick it on a
robot to go and get location,

00:14:17.677 --> 00:14:19.510
how do you think I might
be able to do that?

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:22.690
LAURENCE MORONEY: You
mean something like this?

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:24.690
ANKUR KOTWAL: Yeah,
something exactly like this.

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.981
LAURENCE MORONEY: I bet you
wouldn't give it a nice bow

00:14:26.981 --> 00:14:27.960
tie like this guy has.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:29.400
So if you were to
stick one on there--

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:30.360
I can't start them up now.

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.026
How would you do that,
and how would you

00:14:32.026 --> 00:14:35.400
be able to find his location,
given that he might be indoors,

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.920
given that he doesn't have
GPS, or something like that?

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.940
Well, how you can do that is you
can sense the world around you

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:46.050
for Wi-Fi access points or
for cell tower access points.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:48.930
And when you sense these,
like, we all have a laptop.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:50.989
We open up our laptop,
we look for Wi-Fi.

00:14:50.989 --> 00:14:53.280
You drop down the thing, and
you see the access points,

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:54.447
and you see signal strength.

00:14:54.447 --> 00:14:56.863
It's because the operating
system gives you a service that

00:14:56.863 --> 00:14:58.020
allows you to do that.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.210
In Linux, there's
a WLAN zero scan

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:01.810
command that you
can use to get that,

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.669
and Mac you have a
command for doing it,

00:15:03.669 --> 00:15:05.460
and Windows, you have
a command in Android,

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:07.127
and iOS you have a
command for doing it.

00:15:07.127 --> 00:15:08.835
And what that will do
is it will give you

00:15:08.835 --> 00:15:11.340
a list of access points and
the actual relative signal

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:12.942
strength at that access point.

00:15:12.942 --> 00:15:14.400
Now, if you're
doing that, then you

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.900
can start doing interesting
stuff to triangulate it

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:21.080
because there is a
formula that if you know

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.730
the strength of the signal
from an access point,

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.920
and you know the frequency
on which that access

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.439
point broadcasts, like 2.4
gigahertz or 5 gigahertz,

00:15:29.439 --> 00:15:31.980
you can actually calculate the
estimated distance between you

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:33.380
and that access point.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.310
There's a few things
that can throw it off,

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:37.890
for example, 2.4
gigahertz wireless.

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:40.320
The signal in a 2.4
gigahertz wireless

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.210
tends to get absorbed
by water, so if there's

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:44.050
a lot of water between
you and the target,

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.675
then you get a lot
of loss in strength.

00:15:45.675 --> 00:15:47.610
So I always say, my
pro tip as a Googler,

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.650
if you take nothing away
from this conference,

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.230
if you want to increase the
power of your Wi-Fi at home,

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.697
if you're using 2.4 gigahertz,
just ask your family

00:15:54.697 --> 00:15:57.030
to lie down, and then you'll
get a much stronger signal,

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:58.440
because the water in
their body isn't going

00:15:58.440 --> 00:15:59.523
to be blocking the signal.

00:15:59.523 --> 00:16:00.430
It works every time.

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.010
I've tried it.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:02.880
No I haven't.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:06.810
So anyway, how this works is you
scan for Wi-Fi access points,

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:10.920
and then using the formula
that you can calculate upon,

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:14.340
it will then give you the
estimated distance between you

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:15.542
and that access point.

00:16:15.542 --> 00:16:17.250
Now, if you have that
estimated distance,

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:18.954
that gives you a
radius of a circle.

00:16:18.954 --> 00:16:20.370
It can't tell you
where the access

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:21.990
point is because you don't
know what direction it's

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:22.599
coming from.

00:16:22.599 --> 00:16:24.390
But you know there's
an access point there.

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:25.980
You know you're a
distance from it.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:27.810
So from there, you
can draw a circle.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:29.580
So if you have a
second access point,

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.009
you can draw another circle.

00:16:31.009 --> 00:16:32.550
And if you have a
third access point,

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.150
you can draw another circle.

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:36.510
So the more access points
you're able to scan,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.400
and the more accurate
the signal you're

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.350
able to read or the
signal loss off of these

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:41.730
you're able to
read, then you can

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:43.530
start plotting these circles.

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:47.190
And the overlap of the circles
is where you're likely to be.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:49.680
So at Google, we actually
have a geolocation API

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.990
that does all the heavy
lifting of this for you.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.420
So if you were to scan
your local Wi-Fi access

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.730
points, and that's what
this little robot does

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:58.800
that Ankor was asking about.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.770
So that will scan the
local Wi-Fi access points.

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:04.619
It will get their IDs
that identify them.

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:06.930
It will get the signal
loss off of them.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.490
It will call--
it'll pass that up

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:10.980
to Google with a
simple HTTP call,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.109
so it's one of our
web service APIs.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:14.970
And then the web
service will then

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.089
return a pretty
good guess of where

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:20.160
you are latitude
and longitude wise,

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.669
as well as an accuracy
estimation around that.

00:17:22.669 --> 00:17:24.960
If you remember that red
circle on my previous diagram,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.790
it was a circle,
it wasn't a point.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.634
Because where all of these
circles for signal overlap,

00:17:29.634 --> 00:17:31.550
it's going to give you
a space, and that space

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:33.575
is going to determine
roughly where you are.

00:17:33.575 --> 00:17:34.950
So what you'll
get back from this

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.800
is expected latitude and
longitude, as well as

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.540
an accuracy estimation.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:41.130
The most common
one I've seen when

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.290
I scan it in a
suburban neighborhood

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:45.030
is usually about 20 meters.

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.896
So it's pretty good,
I think, if I'm--

00:17:47.896 --> 00:17:49.770
well, I drive this guy
around my neighborhood

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:50.728
when I was testing him.

00:17:50.728 --> 00:17:53.310
And I was usually within 20
meters of my actual latitude

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.640
and longitude that I detected
using a GPS by doing this,

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.430
just by sniffing the Wi-Fi
signals in my neighborhood.

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:01.263
ANKUR KOTWAL: Now, this
is particularly good

00:18:01.263 --> 00:18:04.260
if in your business, the type
of app that you're building

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:06.090
happens to do some
sort of asset tracking,

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:10.350
and you're in locations
where GPS can't be found.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:14.490
And particularly, as
Laurence referred to earlier,

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:15.490
was the canyon effect.

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:18.030
So in certain cities
with tall buildings,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:20.460
you do lose that
direct line of sight.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.890
But in those urban
environments, there

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.350
are a lot of Wi-Fi access
points that are still

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:31.440
able to help us reliably locate
real time movement of whatever

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.830
asset it is that
you're tracking.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.038
LAURENCE MORONEY: And with
Ankur's Australian accent,

00:18:35.038 --> 00:18:37.890
I see the closed captioning
made it the Kenyan effect rather

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.214
than the canyon effect.

00:18:40.214 --> 00:18:42.800
[LAUGHS] Just if anybody
is reading along.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.470
So here's an example of
how you would use that API.

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.060
So here's where
I've done a scan.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:50.190
I've gotten a couple
of Wi-Fi access points.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.170
I see the signal
strength off of them.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:54.260
When you scan for
Wi-Fi, all of the APIs

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.950
will give you back
a signal strength.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.480
That's how your
phone knows when you

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.790
see the little visualization,
usually of three bars,

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:02.060
or your laptop knows you
get that visualization.

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:03.510
It's off of the signal strength.

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.360
A good signal strength
is between negative 30

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:08.010
and negative 60 decibels.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:09.680
If you're below
negative 60, you're

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:11.013
going to start having drop outs.

00:19:11.013 --> 00:19:13.750
If you're above negative 30,
it's probably lying to you.

00:19:13.750 --> 00:19:16.810
And so you'll get a signal, or
you're sitting on top of it.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.180
You'll get some data like this.

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:19.980
You'll create a
json file out of it.

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.750
You'll call the back end API,
and then the back end API

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.490
will return json like that
on the right hand side.

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:28.180
Just as an example, for a
device built using this,

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.100
I have this little guy
that I've been showing.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:32.260
And this is a
little pet tracker.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:34.180
And what this has,
instead of-- so

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:36.670
if you've got a tiny little
dog like I do, instead

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:39.190
of you needing a full GPS
circuit to be able to see where

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:41.980
your dog is at all times,
this guy has a 2G cell,

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.850
has Wi-Fi and has a BLE, so I
can find my dog wherever he is.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:47.500
But unfortunately,
if he's lost now,

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.140
he's lost, because I
have it in my pocket.

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:53.500
So with that in mind,
you're able to have

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:57.300
all of these tools, from GPS
all the way down to, if there

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:59.560
is no GPS available,
to scan Wi-Fi,

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:02.009
to scan cell services,
to know where

00:20:02.009 --> 00:20:03.550
you are on the
surface of the planet,

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.500
to get a latitude
and a longitude

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.771
with reasonable accuracy
for where you are.

00:20:07.771 --> 00:20:09.520
And that's nice, and
that's all very good.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:12.880
But we don't live in a
latitude and longitude, right?

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.590
We generally live in an address.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:16.690
And when civilization
comes to an environment,

00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:19.150
and infrastructure is
built and roads are built,

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:22.660
and houses are built, the way
of encoding, routing something

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.060
to a particular location
is through an address.

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:28.210
But addresses have existed
long before Google Maps.

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:31.150
And addresses have existed
long before computers and all

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:32.240
of these kind of things.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.810
And as a result, there's all
of these wildly diverging

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:36.700
standards of address.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:40.510
So I live in the Seattle area,
and we have these horrendously

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:41.680
complicated addresses.

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:44.819
But like everybody else in
the US, we have a zip code.

00:20:44.819 --> 00:20:47.110
So if you're filling something
out, you get a zip code.

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:49.510
But I grew up in Ireland,
and I grew up in the UK,

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:52.120
and over there, we have
postcodes instead of zip codes.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.520
And there's some encoding
in the postcodes as well.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.600
Like, OK.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:56.590
Trivia question.

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.660
My zip code used to
begin with the letter CF.

00:20:58.660 --> 00:21:01.852
Can you guess what
city I lived in?

00:21:01.852 --> 00:21:03.060
Not many British people here.

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:03.520
OK.

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:04.020
What?

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:04.635
What's that?

00:21:04.635 --> 00:21:05.760
AUDIENCE: It was Cambridge.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:06.640
LAURENCE MORONEY:
It was Cardiff.

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:07.630
So close, close.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:08.710
Pretty good.

00:21:08.710 --> 00:21:10.750
It was actually encoded
in the postcode.

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:13.000
And I think Australian
postcodes are different, right?

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:13.840
ANKUR KOTWAL: Australian
postcodes are different.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:15.150
Again, if you look at--

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:18.130
so in America, it's a zip
code, which is fully numeric.

00:21:18.130 --> 00:21:20.020
In the UK, it's
a postcode, which

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.880
you've got letters and numbers.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:24.010
Australia decided to take
a little bit of both.

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:26.320
And so we have
postcodes like the UK,

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:30.010
but we have fully numeric
postcodes like the US.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:31.960
So there is this inconsistency.

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:36.130
And if you're a business that
has to do global delivery,

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.950
you need to understand that
your user's address, you

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:42.640
need to have a good way of being
able to route whatever package

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.770
it is to them,
and be comfortable

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:48.065
that it's going to get there
on time rather than get lost.

00:21:48.065 --> 00:21:49.690
LAURENCE MORONEY: So
there's no letters

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:51.385
in the Australian postcode.

00:21:51.385 --> 00:21:51.880
ANKUR KOTWAL: That is correct.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:53.200
LAURENCE MORONEY: So what
does the postman deliver then

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.046
if you have no letters?

00:21:55.046 --> 00:21:58.160
ANKUR KOTWAL: Move
on, Laurence, move on.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:01.126
It didn't work during rehearsal,
I don't think it works now.

00:22:01.126 --> 00:22:01.800
[LAUGHS]

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:02.680
LAURENCE MORONEY:
But from the point

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.420
of view as a software
developer, there's

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:07.630
a lot of complexity
in handling addresses.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:09.340
Street names are
structured differently,

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.806
house numbers are
structured differently.

00:22:11.806 --> 00:22:13.180
When I was growing
up in Ireland,

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:15.160
and I see American
addresses, where you've got

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:16.930
houses in the 35 thousands--

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:21.100
I couldn't understand how a
house could have number 35,000.

00:22:21.100 --> 00:22:23.920
The biggest house number
we had was probably 99.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.920
And so addresses are very
different all over the world.

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:30.190
It's very difficult
to get them right.

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:32.232
It's very easy for an end
user to get them wrong,

00:22:32.232 --> 00:22:34.481
particularly if you're typing
on a phone, where you've

00:22:34.481 --> 00:22:35.880
got a tiny little keyboard.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.710
So tools to make a
software developer's life

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.530
who is building something where
people enter addresses easier

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:44.230
are paramount, and that's why we
have things such as the address

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:45.860
autocomplete in Google Maps.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:48.820
So as you can see here
that somebody was typing in

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:53.139
Sydney Number 1 Marti, and in
Sydney, there's a Martin Place,

00:22:53.139 --> 00:22:55.180
there's a Martin Avenue,
there's a Martin Street,

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:56.929
and there's a Martin
Street in Paddington,

00:22:56.929 --> 00:22:58.872
and there's a 1 Martin Place.

00:22:58.872 --> 00:23:00.580
So there's all of
these different things,

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:03.760
but just by typing
1 Marti, this person

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.102
is now given the ability
to pick from one of these.

00:23:06.102 --> 00:23:07.810
And then when they
pick from one of them,

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:09.268
they know that the
address is going

00:23:09.268 --> 00:23:10.425
to be formatted correctly.

00:23:10.425 --> 00:23:12.550
And in this case, they're
all Australian addresses,

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:14.470
so the postcode probably doesn't
have any letters in them,

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:15.360
right?

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.010
And as a result, if
you're building a service,

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:20.350
or if you are building
an application that

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:24.250
needs to route parcels or
needs to route details of cars

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:27.670
or people or anything, then
this reduces the friction

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:30.070
of that greatly, as well
as greatly increasing

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.840
the user experience
because nobody

00:23:31.840 --> 00:23:34.094
wants to build an
online shopping service,

00:23:34.094 --> 00:23:36.010
and then you deliver
goods to the wrong people

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:41.290
because they haven't entered
the addresses correctly.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:42.764
So how do you do this?

00:23:42.764 --> 00:23:43.680
How do you build this?

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.604
So just a quick example
in an Android application.

00:23:46.604 --> 00:23:48.520
So there's an address
order complete activity,

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:50.530
and there's an address
order complete fragment.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:52.550
In this case, I'm just
showing the fragment.

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:53.860
Here there is a
fragment, and you just

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:54.850
give the fragment the name.

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.170
I've abbreviated in the code.

00:23:56.170 --> 00:23:59.549
That's
com.google.android.gms.location

00:23:59.549 --> 00:24:01.840
at the bottom, and then there
is the place autocomplete

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:02.620
fragment.

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:04.520
You define a fragment
such as this,

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.040
and then you use your fragment.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:08.980
And when you use the fragment,
the autocomplete fragment,

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.100
upon the user having picked
something from that list

00:24:12.100 --> 00:24:15.850
that I showed a moment ago,
the setOnPlaceSelectedListener

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.450
will fire the
onPlaceSelected event.

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.350
So that onPlaceSelected
event will

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:23.950
return an object called a place,
and that place object then

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:26.170
has all the metadata
that you need

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:28.847
associated with that address,
including the address.

00:24:28.847 --> 00:24:31.180
But there's lots of other
good stuff that's in the place

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.070
that we're going to get
to in just a moment.

00:24:33.070 --> 00:24:34.647
Actually, why don't we
get to it right now?

00:24:34.647 --> 00:24:35.438
ANKUR KOTWAL: Yeah.

00:24:35.438 --> 00:24:38.542
So just to call out that auto
complete widget one more time,

00:24:38.542 --> 00:24:40.750
it's available on the web,
it's available on Android,

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:42.430
and it's available on iOS.

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:44.260
And I hope the value
is really clear.

00:24:44.260 --> 00:24:46.850
It's really simple to
use for a developer.

00:24:46.850 --> 00:24:50.170
You get a reliable address
input that you can definitely

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:52.240
start routing your
deliveries to,

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:54.440
or whatever it is
that you need to do.

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:56.440
And from a user
standpoint, you've really

00:24:56.440 --> 00:24:58.600
reduced that friction,
and you've effectively

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:03.130
increased confidence in
the service for both sides.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.120
Really, really handy.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:10.490
Now, here, this is a
picture of a theme park.

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:13.370
When we recall our visits
to our theme parks,

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:16.667
we think of Disneyland,
Universal Studios,

00:25:16.667 --> 00:25:18.500
whichever theme park
it is that you went to.

00:25:18.500 --> 00:25:22.100
Again, we don't think of
these places in addresses.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:24.340
We don't think of them
even at a city level.

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:28.220
We think of them, actually,
as a semantic place.

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:32.630
And for that very purpose,
we built out the Places API.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:36.200
Because if you remember
back to that graph

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:39.050
I showed you earlier of
location intelligence

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:41.660
and building it up, one
of the fundamental parts

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.690
of that is that we needed
to understand the world

00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:46.490
as our users understood it.

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:48.860
We needed to build
a good understanding

00:25:48.860 --> 00:25:52.550
of the urban environments, of
the way people would look up

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:55.670
addresses or establishments.

00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:59.870
And so Places is a database,
a point of interest database

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.450
that we have that has
over 100 million places

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:04.580
all over the world.

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:09.080
And we use many mechanisms
to build this database out

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:09.920
and keep it current.

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:12.840
So let me show you a
quick couple of examples.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.600
One of the ways that we're able
to build these Places database

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:17.600
out is just by looking
at satellite photos,

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:20.900
and we're able to identify
building outlines and roofs

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:25.550
and so on so that we can see
sort of where a place finishes

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:28.450
or exactly its
location on the planet

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:30.930
and delineate it
from the next one.

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.370
So satellite photos are one
way that we can do this.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:37.070
Another way we could do
it is just through even

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.430
our consumer app, Google Maps.

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:42.260
So here, this is
Shoreline Amphitheater.

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:45.789
It's just behind the Google
Campus in Mountain View.

00:26:45.789 --> 00:26:47.330
And what you are
starting to see here

00:26:47.330 --> 00:26:48.980
is that we're actually editing.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:50.840
We're able to suggest edits.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:54.500
So we're able to have our users
provide us with information

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.430
if it happens to be
incomplete or inaccurate.

00:26:57.430 --> 00:26:59.950
They can suggest a name;
they can suggest an address.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.510
And there's some additional
metadata there as well.

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:05.360
People have rated the place.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.207
This is the kind of
location intelligence

00:27:07.207 --> 00:27:08.040
we're talking about.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:11.180
There's ratings, there's photos,
you can go to Street View,

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:13.070
so on and so forth.

00:27:13.070 --> 00:27:15.470
We of course have the
underlying metadata

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:18.590
that we need from our latitude
and longitude standpoint,

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:20.210
but what I want to
really highlight

00:27:20.210 --> 00:27:23.270
is that we have very user
visible and user relevant

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:25.950
information there.

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:28.410
So suggesting edits
through Google Maps

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:31.540
is another way that we're able
to gather some of this data.

00:27:31.540 --> 00:27:34.660
We also have an API call
the Google My Business API.

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.320
So if you have a
business, one way

00:27:37.320 --> 00:27:39.666
to get it listed onto Google
Maps is to use this API.

00:27:39.666 --> 00:27:41.040
And it's particularly
useful when

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.054
you've got lots of storefronts.

00:27:44.054 --> 00:27:45.720
And so basically, you
can go in, and you

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:48.000
can provide all of this
data and keep it current.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:50.160
And it's, of course,
every business,

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.600
it's in their best
interest to do so.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:54.459
We also rely on Google
Search to provide us

00:27:54.459 --> 00:27:55.500
some of this information.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:28:01.140
So Google Search does a really
good job of indexing storefront

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.600
websites to make sure that
things like opening hours

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:04.597
are current.

00:28:04.597 --> 00:28:06.180
And for those of you
that might think,

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:07.980
how often do opening
hours change,

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:09.630
it's actually quite frequently.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:11.940
Think about all of the
public holidays that happen,

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.080
when stores are closed.

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:15.950
Holiday season, when stores tend
to be open for longer hours,

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:18.494
so people can do their
holiday shopping.

00:28:18.494 --> 00:28:20.910
This is the kind of information
that does change from time

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:21.760
to time.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:25.990
And the Places API is a great
way to get that information.

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:27.990
So once we have this
Places database,

00:28:27.990 --> 00:28:30.740
the way that we're
able to surface it

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:33.780
is through the Google
Maps app or website

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:35.250
and through the Places API.

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:37.110
Because we want to
make this information

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.200
available to developers so
that you can build innovative

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:42.770
apps on top of it.

00:28:42.770 --> 00:28:46.470
Now, the Places API, it really
aims to answer three things,

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:48.720
or help with three things.

00:28:48.720 --> 00:28:50.600
One is to help
your users realize

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:52.810
where they are, to locate them.

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:54.280
And if you look at
this whole talk,

00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:56.890
so far, we've been talking
very much about locations.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:59.260
Because when a user thinks
about where are they,

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:01.690
they're not looking for
a latitude, longitude.

00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:05.020
They're not looking for
an address, in most cases.

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:08.350
They are literally looking
for the establishment.

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.840
They also want to be able to
tell what is at that place,

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:13.660
so it's great for doing some
discovery ahead of time,

00:29:13.660 --> 00:29:16.249
but also discovery
around them, around

00:29:16.249 --> 00:29:17.290
that particular location.

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.600
The thing that ties places
together is this thing.

00:29:25.600 --> 00:29:27.520
It's called the place ID.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:31.510
Every single place in the
world has a unique place ID.

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:33.190
And place IDs are
not just unique,

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.580
but they're stable,
so they don't change.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:37.330
And if for whatever
reason, a business

00:29:37.330 --> 00:29:40.660
happens to close
down or move, we're

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:43.390
able to take that
developer experience

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:45.670
and keep it actually
really strong.

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.400
So if a business wants
to move locations,

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:51.010
we will still
return your results

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.300
for the new place based
on its old place ID,

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:58.060
and we'll give you an
updated place ID as well.

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:00.550
And of course, with
Places, the other advantage

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:03.040
is that it has global coverage.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:06.250
And when we think of a place,
it's any number of things.

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:06.820
Right?

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:11.410
It could be something as simple
as road segments, an address,

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:12.220
or even shops.

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:17.930
So cafes, bars, libraries,
schools, places of worship.

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:19.730
In fact, in Moscone
Center, we're

00:30:19.730 --> 00:30:23.000
able to even tell you
the difference in places

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:25.010
between level two
and level three.

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:28.700
And you can start to see some
really interesting use cases.

00:30:28.700 --> 00:30:33.110
Places help us solve that
indoor mapping problem, right?

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:34.940
Because with indoor
mapping, you don't

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:39.200
have GPS to rely on, so you
get a very inaccurate latitude,

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.550
longitude.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.890
Even addresses are
somewhat limited

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:45.600
when you're talking
about indoor mapping,

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:48.630
because you might have
the address for the mall

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:52.470
that you're at rather
than a particular cafe

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:54.390
that you happen to
be within that mall.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:56.098
LAURENCE MORONEY: And
then some addresses

00:30:56.098 --> 00:30:58.770
can have different layers
encoded in the address.

00:30:58.770 --> 00:31:02.310
Like there's 221B
Baker Street, which

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:04.035
we're all familiar with, right?

00:31:04.035 --> 00:31:06.180
ANKUR KOTWAL: Who knows
what 221B Baker Street is?

00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:07.019
LAURENCE MORONEY: Oh, good.

00:31:07.019 --> 00:31:08.160
ANKUR KOTWAL: A few people.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:08.550
Great.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:09.400
LAURENCE MORONEY: Yes.

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:10.515
But that's just an example.

00:31:10.515 --> 00:31:12.348
Like, if you have an
apartment split in two,

00:31:12.348 --> 00:31:14.840
like 221 Baker Street
and 221B Baker Street,

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:18.120
221B is very famous, but 221
isn't quite so famous, right?

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.910
So with the ability
to be able to separate

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:24.690
into unique locations like
that, then the address of 221B

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:27.690
would be considered a separate
location, a separate place.

00:31:27.690 --> 00:31:28.290
ANKUR KOTWAL: So
full disclosure,

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:29.664
I didn't know what
it was, and we

00:31:29.664 --> 00:31:31.610
are going to find out,
because Laurence is

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:33.152
going to tell us in a minute.

00:31:33.152 --> 00:31:35.527
LAURENCE MORONEY: I tried to
get the hat, but I couldn't.

00:31:35.527 --> 00:31:38.110
ANKUR KOTWAL: Location sharing
is also a very important action

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:39.080
that users take, right?

00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:41.640
It happens a lot more often
than you might realize.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:44.460
If you think about your
day to day conversations

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:47.320
with your friends or your
family, you could be saying,

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:48.400
hey, where are you?

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:49.165
I'm here.

00:31:49.165 --> 00:31:50.040
Are you running late?

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:52.260
Yes, I'm stuck in
traffic at this point,

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:54.400
or this particular
train is running late,

00:31:54.400 --> 00:31:56.207
I'm at this station.

00:31:56.207 --> 00:31:58.290
And location sharing is
also one of those things--

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:01.830
as a developer, it's not
always easy to get right.

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:03.780
Because as I said,
there's different sources

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.610
of information that
you can rely on,

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:09.450
and just providing a latitude,
longitude or an address

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:10.710
isn't enough.

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:12.947
Places, however, are
a much better way

00:32:12.947 --> 00:32:14.280
to actually share your location.

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:16.210
Because again, it's
a semantic place.

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:19.507
It's how we naturally
think of our location.

00:32:19.507 --> 00:32:21.090
So what you're seeing
here is actually

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:24.000
our place picker, which
is a UI widget that is

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.490
available on Android and iOS.

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:29.350
And what it does is when you
go to share your location,

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:31.440
if you present the
place picker, it

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.281
shows you a nice map with a
bunch of places around you.

00:32:35.281 --> 00:32:36.780
Because you're using
indoor mapping,

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:40.320
we give you a list of
locations that you could be at,

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.570
and we sort that list
based on the likelihood

00:32:42.570 --> 00:32:45.030
that you are actually
at that place.

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:47.730
And the example where that
becomes very important,

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:49.980
the sort order of that
list, is that we're

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.310
able to apply some of this
location intelligence.

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:57.540
If you're at a bus stop that
happens to be close to a cafe,

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:00.030
but you're at that
bus stop at 6:00 PM,

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.720
and that cafe closed at
5:00 PM, it's unlikely

00:33:03.720 --> 00:33:05.800
that you're in that cafe.

00:33:05.800 --> 00:33:07.770
And so we're able
to dynamically sort

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:11.160
that list in our place picker,
the likelihood of places

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:12.420
that you're at.

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:16.770
We can change that likelihood
based on real world

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:17.990
information in real time.

00:33:20.814 --> 00:33:22.480
Now, I just showed
you the place picker,

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:23.830
which is a drop-in element.

00:33:23.830 --> 00:33:26.560
But if you wanted to go ahead
and get the place yourself

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:28.000
programmatically,
like, basically

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:30.610
show no user interface,
here's some Android code

00:33:30.610 --> 00:33:31.960
on how you would do that.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:34.690
At the top, you can see that
we call this method, called

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:38.260
getCurrentPlace, and it's part
of the place detection API

00:33:38.260 --> 00:33:40.140
namespace.

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:42.495
We also have a call
back method in this,

00:33:42.495 --> 00:33:43.960
so it's an asynchronous call.

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:45.960
But when it goes ahead
and returns those places,

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:47.550
it gives us this buffer.

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:49.620
It's a list of likely
places, and they're

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:52.440
sorted in likelihood order.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:54.940
And because it's this place
object, we can just look at it

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:56.560
and get the likelihood.

00:33:56.560 --> 00:34:00.520
We can get its name, and most
importantly, we can get its ID.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:03.760
The place ID is really,
really important

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:06.940
because it helps us
retrieve this place again

00:34:06.940 --> 00:34:08.639
when we need to.

00:34:08.639 --> 00:34:12.080
So one thing I want to
make clear about places

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:17.455
is if you use places in your
apps, hold on to the place ID.

00:34:17.455 --> 00:34:19.080
Don't hold on to the
other details that

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:21.420
come on with the
rest of the place--

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:24.060
the name, the opening hours,
the address, because that's

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:27.152
information that could
change over time.

00:34:27.152 --> 00:34:28.610
We also have some
terms of service,

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:30.734
because we want to make
sure that that data remains

00:34:30.734 --> 00:34:31.969
fresh in your apps.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:33.469
So the thing you
want to hold on to,

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:35.171
the thing you want
to cache, or--

00:34:35.171 --> 00:34:36.170
LAURENCE MORONEY: Cache.

00:34:36.170 --> 00:34:38.360
Like the rest of us.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:40.190
ANKUR KOTWAL: Is the
place ID, because it

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:41.719
means that you have
that one point,

00:34:41.719 --> 00:34:43.760
that primary key
that you need to be

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:47.750
able to resolve to whichever
place you want in a future

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:49.448
look up.

00:34:49.448 --> 00:34:50.989
And we've just told
you three things.

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:53.280
We talked about lat-long,
we've talked about addresses,

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:54.600
we've talked about places.

00:34:54.600 --> 00:34:56.141
Laurence is now
going to tell you how

00:34:56.141 --> 00:34:57.470
we can bring this all together.

00:34:57.470 --> 00:35:00.180
LAURENCE MORONEY: Sure, so I
like to think about it as a--

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:00.630
ANKUR KOTWAL: Oh, sorry.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:01.537
LAURENCE MORONEY: You forgot.

00:35:01.537 --> 00:35:02.870
ANKUR KOTWAL: Oh, I missed it.

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:03.920
My bad.

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:05.350
LAURENCE MORONEY: So excited
to get me back on stage.

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:07.010
ANKUR KOTWAL: I'm so excited
to get you back on stage.

00:35:07.010 --> 00:35:09.470
I'm going to give you a
little bit more about places,

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:11.520
and that is the
kind of information

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:12.770
that you can get about places.

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.850
So once you have that place
ID, and you return to a place,

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:17.660
see this information
you see here

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:19.551
from Google Maps, the
actual consumer app?

00:35:19.551 --> 00:35:21.050
This is the same
type of information

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:22.425
that you can use
in your own apps

00:35:22.425 --> 00:35:23.910
with the place object itself.

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:26.930
So we have the name, we have the
address, website, phone number,

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:27.720
so on.

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:29.191
But we also have ratings.

00:35:29.191 --> 00:35:29.690
Right?

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:31.460
We have photos that
you can bring in,

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:35.150
so that when you want to
present a place to your users,

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:36.890
you can show it in
a rich, visual way.

00:35:36.890 --> 00:35:39.530
It doesn't have to be on a map.

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:44.750
You can even present the place
type that this place is at.

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:46.580
So if you don't want
to present bars,

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:49.610
for example, if it's an app
that's targeted for a younger

00:35:49.610 --> 00:35:51.410
audience, or a bar
just happens to be

00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:53.660
an inappropriate type of
place you want to show,

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:56.960
you can just not present that.

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:00.240
And then for users that are
doing that sort of discovery,

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:03.130
price level is really
important because you

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:05.130
can get into a situation
where users are looking

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:06.671
for a restaurant,
and they're looking

00:36:06.671 --> 00:36:11.760
for something that meets
their needs in a better way.

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:15.450
When you hold on
to a place ID, you

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:18.630
can return that place object
by calling a method called

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:20.087
get place by ID.

00:36:20.087 --> 00:36:22.545
And so what we do here is,
again it's an asynchronous call.

00:36:22.545 --> 00:36:25.602
But when we get that call
back, the on result call back,

00:36:25.602 --> 00:36:27.060
the first thing
we're doing is just

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:28.829
validating that we
got a place back.

00:36:28.829 --> 00:36:30.870
And the only time when
you won't get a place back

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:34.230
is if you pasted an invalid
place ID, for example.

00:36:34.230 --> 00:36:38.190
But we go and validate that we
got a place back for our ID.

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:40.050
And then once we have
that place object,

00:36:40.050 --> 00:36:41.633
we can do all sorts
of things with it.

00:36:41.633 --> 00:36:43.810
Like I said, we have
the name, the address,

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.570
and all of this metadata
around that place.

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:47.980
It's immensely powerful.

00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:50.370
But the place ID
happens to be the key

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:53.862
that you want to hold on
to to use the Places API.

00:36:53.862 --> 00:36:55.320
Now I'll definitely
give it to you.

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:56.486
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thank you.

00:36:56.486 --> 00:36:58.380
So again, thinking
in terms of there's

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:00.930
all of these separate concepts.

00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:03.330
There's latitude and
longitude for plotting

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:05.210
where you are on the
surface of the globe,

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:08.460
there's addresses for routing
to where your house is, there's

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:12.120
places, and in this case, it's
a unique and distinct Google

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:15.090
thing, where we have an ID
associated with a place,

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:18.242
and we have all the metadata
associated with that place.

00:37:18.242 --> 00:37:19.950
How do we make all of
this work together?

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.526
And we like to think about
this in terms of a stack.

00:37:22.526 --> 00:37:24.150
We're developers,
engineers, after all.

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:25.930
Ultimately,
everything's a stack.

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:27.720
So starting with the
surface of the planet

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:30.190
and all those
points on it, those

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:31.860
determined to get
some intelligence

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:33.780
about those points, you
need the coordinates.

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:35.430
You need to know
where that point is.

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:37.290
You need your latitude
and your longitude.

00:37:37.290 --> 00:37:39.180
And as a result, we've
got a bunch of APIs

00:37:39.180 --> 00:37:41.430
for that, as I
showed earlier on.

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.590
Within the operating
system level itself,

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.080
you've got things like fused
location provider in Android

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:48.030
or core location in iOS.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.640
But then on top of this to be
able to get some intelligence,

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.830
like things like the
geolocation APIs,

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:54.960
so that if you can't
get a GPS signal,

00:37:54.960 --> 00:37:57.690
we have a service that you'll be
able to determine where you are

00:37:57.690 --> 00:37:59.470
as a location on the planet.

00:37:59.470 --> 00:38:03.090
Then when it comes to addresses,
so that the next step up,

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:05.460
once civilization has
reached that location,

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:06.990
addresses become important.

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:09.480
And we want to give you some
tools that will make it easier

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:11.890
for you to be able to use
addresses in your application,

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:14.490
so that you get the right
address with a great user

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:16.590
experience, instead of
doing lots of typing,

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:17.670
and you reduce errors.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:20.400
And that's what the
autocomplete API was all about.

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:22.110
And then, as Ankur
mentioned, places.

00:38:22.110 --> 00:38:25.380
And places, for me, is when it
really begins to get exciting.

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:29.806
Because I want to know about
the world around me, and it's

00:38:29.806 --> 00:38:31.680
hard for me to know
about the world around me

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:34.220
by looking at the numbers
of a latitude or longitude

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:35.310
or looking at address.

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:37.770
Sometimes the address
has a famous implication.

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:39.540
221B Baker Street, I go, OK.

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:41.260
I can get excited about that.

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:45.490
But if it's 123 Letsy Avenue,
I have no idea what that means.

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.430
And as a result then,
there's place metadata

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.990
that's built on top of this.

00:38:48.990 --> 00:38:50.970
So with the place
metadata, I can now

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:55.560
start seeing things such
as reviews, prices, photos,

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:56.760
and all of these things.

00:38:56.760 --> 00:38:59.010
And then once I have all of
this data-- and this place

00:38:59.010 --> 00:39:01.230
ID is the thing that
ties it all together--

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:03.000
then there's a bunch
of other services

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:06.360
that I can start using,
such as the Directions API.

00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:09.280
So if I know a place
that I want to go to,

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:11.730
and if I know where I am,
then I can get directions

00:39:11.730 --> 00:39:13.680
to go to where I want to go.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:15.445
And I can get turn
by turn directions.

00:39:15.445 --> 00:39:17.070
I can get them by
transit, I can get it

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:19.075
by walking, by
biking, by driving,

00:39:19.075 --> 00:39:20.200
and all that kind of stuff.

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:22.890
So I wanted to come up
with an idea for a scenario

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:26.500
where I would bring
all of these together.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:29.630
So have you ever
traveled to a city,

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:33.450
and you knew there was something
you wanted to see in that city,

00:39:33.450 --> 00:39:36.510
but you only had the smallest
piece of information about it?

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:39.120
And you might go up to the
hotel concierge and go,

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:43.050
is there a cafe called
Speedy's around here somewhere?

00:39:43.050 --> 00:39:46.230
And the concierge
would have no idea,

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:48.900
because you just have that
tiny, tiny piece of information.

00:39:48.900 --> 00:39:51.240
So I thought, what if you
could build a service that

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:54.510
builds upon all of this
location intelligence

00:39:54.510 --> 00:39:56.760
to be able to discover
things like that for you,

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.747
like a digital
concierge, as it were?

00:39:58.747 --> 00:40:01.080
So I'm going to step through
how the APIs could do that.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:03.750
So you've probably
established by now

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:05.460
that I'm a huge fan
of Sherlock Holmes,

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:07.680
and that's where 221B
Baker Street comes from.

00:40:07.680 --> 00:40:10.590
And any of you watch the
modern BBC version of Sherlock?

00:40:10.590 --> 00:40:12.000
Isn't it brilliant?

00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:12.870
OK.

00:40:12.870 --> 00:40:15.840
Now, I travel to London
once or twice a year,

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:17.760
and most of "Sherlock"
is filmed in Cardiff,

00:40:17.760 --> 00:40:19.170
where I used to live.

00:40:19.170 --> 00:40:21.555
But when I last
traveled to London,

00:40:21.555 --> 00:40:22.680
it was about two years ago.

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:24.480
I was in London,
and I really wanted

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:27.510
to know where they filmed
the scenes of 221B Baker

00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:30.090
Street because they didn't
film them on Baker Street.

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:32.517
Because I know the area, and
that's not where they filmed.

00:40:32.517 --> 00:40:33.600
So I was thinking through.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:35.979
It's like, how
could I figure out

00:40:35.979 --> 00:40:38.520
where these scenes are filmed,
so that I can go and visit it,

00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:39.724
and I can geek out.

00:40:39.724 --> 00:40:41.140
So let me give an
example of that.

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:42.390
So if we switch to the laptop.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:45.850
OK.

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:48.670
So say I'm staying in a
hotel in downtown London,

00:40:48.670 --> 00:40:50.650
and I've watched
episodes of "Sherlock,"

00:40:50.650 --> 00:40:53.800
and I know where they film
it, there's a cafe next door.

00:40:53.800 --> 00:40:57.100
And there's a very distinctive
cafe, and it's called Speedy's.

00:40:57.100 --> 00:40:59.350
So whenever you're watching
an episode of the TV show,

00:40:59.350 --> 00:41:00.850
and you see, as they're coming
in and out of the house,

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:02.290
you'll see Speedy's Cafe.

00:41:02.290 --> 00:41:04.360
And that's the only
piece of intelligence

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:07.810
I have about where this
might actually be filmed.

00:41:07.810 --> 00:41:10.840
So using the nearby
search API, what I can do

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:12.820
is I know my latitude
and longitude

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:14.560
of where I am right now.

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:15.490
And I say, OK.

00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:20.440
Can I search for a place
in London within 10 miles

00:41:20.440 --> 00:41:23.260
of my latitude and
longitude, called Speedy's.

00:41:23.260 --> 00:41:25.780
So I did this search, based
on the hotel that I usually

00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:26.770
stay in.

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:29.300
And it came up, and
it found two places.

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:32.220
So if I scroll down, and I
take a look, it's kind of--

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:33.640
sorry if it's hard to see.

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:37.570
But one of them was called
Speedy Sandwich Bar and Cafe,

00:41:37.570 --> 00:41:42.051
and one of them was
called Speedy Noodle.

00:41:42.051 --> 00:41:42.550
OK.

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:45.530
So now, I don't think
Mycroft and Sherlock are

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:47.650
noodle kind of
guys, so I figured

00:41:47.650 --> 00:41:51.460
that it must be Speedy's
Sandwich and Cafe.

00:41:51.460 --> 00:41:52.810
So I thought, OK.

00:41:52.810 --> 00:41:54.280
So if I was building
an application

00:41:54.280 --> 00:41:55.870
to do this kind
of thing, then it

00:41:55.870 --> 00:41:58.090
would give me Speedy
Sandwich Bar and Cafe,

00:41:58.090 --> 00:42:00.610
and that has a place
ID associated with it.

00:42:00.610 --> 00:42:02.800
And it also has map
attributes and all those kinds

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:04.520
of things associated with it.

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:07.480
So I could call
the place details

00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:09.975
for the place ID
for Speedy's Cafe.

00:42:09.975 --> 00:42:11.350
And then in this
place details, I

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:13.780
get a whole bunch of rich
information coming back

00:42:13.780 --> 00:42:14.830
about it.

00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:17.380
And I'm like, but say if I
was building this service,

00:42:17.380 --> 00:42:19.367
that somebody is trying
to find something,

00:42:19.367 --> 00:42:21.700
he says, all he knows is that
it's called Speedy's Cafe.

00:42:21.700 --> 00:42:24.700
Wouldn't it be cool that it
says, I think I've found it.

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:27.250
Let me give you some
pictures so you can verify.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:31.810
So a call to the
place details API

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:34.060
would give me back
something like this.

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:35.865
It would give me
back a map reference.

00:42:35.865 --> 00:42:37.240
And on the map
reference, it also

00:42:37.240 --> 00:42:39.020
gives me back some pictures.

00:42:39.020 --> 00:42:41.380
And here, these
pictures, this was

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:43.690
you URI that was
embedded within that json

00:42:43.690 --> 00:42:44.980
that I just showed you.

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:47.860
These pictures then show me this
picture of this Speedy's Cafe.

00:42:47.860 --> 00:42:51.400
And this was one of
our local explorers,

00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:54.340
called Eleanora, who took this
picture and uploaded it to it.

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:57.316
And if you've ever watched the
show, does this look like it?

00:42:57.316 --> 00:42:58.690
Does this look
like the location?

00:42:58.690 --> 00:42:59.315
It does, right?

00:42:59.315 --> 00:43:01.482
And you can-- there's
even other photographs,

00:43:01.482 --> 00:43:02.440
and you can see inside.

00:43:02.440 --> 00:43:05.450
And if you look closely, you
can see where they filmed.

00:43:05.450 --> 00:43:06.967
And as you keep
going around here,

00:43:06.967 --> 00:43:08.050
you can start geeking out.

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:09.883
You can see Sherlock
getting out of his car,

00:43:09.883 --> 00:43:11.050
right beside Speedy's Cafe.

00:43:11.050 --> 00:43:13.180
So yes, I found the location.

00:43:13.180 --> 00:43:15.790
All I knew was that it
was filmed at a location,

00:43:15.790 --> 00:43:17.420
and it was a
Speedy's Cafe there.

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:21.670
And now I can go on, I can geek
out, and I can go to the cafe

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:24.490
where Sherlock and
Mycroft and Moriarty

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:26.590
drink tea and plan on
conquering the world.

00:43:26.590 --> 00:43:29.272
But wait a minute,
is it open now?

00:43:29.272 --> 00:43:29.980
Well, guess what?

00:43:29.980 --> 00:43:33.000
The place details will tell
me if it's open now or not.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.757
So again, if I go into
the place details,

00:43:34.757 --> 00:43:35.840
we'll see that there's a--

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:37.465
first of all, there's
a flag that says,

00:43:37.465 --> 00:43:40.040
open now equals true,
that's a dead giveaway.

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:42.214
But otherwise, it also
tells me the opening hours.

00:43:42.214 --> 00:43:44.130
As you can see, as I
scroll through this json,

00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:46.580
on any particular day,
it tells me when it opens

00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:47.780
and when it closes.

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:48.860
So this is great.

00:43:48.860 --> 00:43:51.260
I know that this is the
location of Speedy's Cafe.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:52.587
I know that it's open now.

00:43:52.587 --> 00:43:54.920
I know I can go and hang out
with Sherlock and the boys.

00:43:54.920 --> 00:43:56.791
Well, how do I get there?

00:43:56.791 --> 00:43:57.290
All right.

00:43:57.290 --> 00:43:58.340
Well, I'm in London.

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:01.130
I don't have a car, so I'm
relying on public transport.

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:05.240
So there's yet another API,
called the directions API.

00:44:05.240 --> 00:44:07.700
And if you look really
closely at the URI here--

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:09.320
I know it's a little bit small--

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:11.690
but when I started,
I was in a hotel.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:14.930
I used my GPS, and I got my
latitude and my longitude.

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:16.400
But when I did all
this searching,

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:18.950
and I matched against these
pictures, and I got a cafe,

00:44:18.950 --> 00:44:22.054
I got a place ID, and it was
the place ID of that cafe.

00:44:22.054 --> 00:44:23.720
So if you look at the
URI, in my origin,

00:44:23.720 --> 00:44:25.190
I'm giving a latitude
and longitude

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:27.650
because that's what I starting
with, and at my destination,

00:44:27.650 --> 00:44:29.310
I'm giving a place ID.

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:31.820
So I can call the API,
and I get details back.

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:34.280
And I say to the API,
well, I'm not in a car.

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:36.017
I'm in London,
can I use transit?

00:44:36.017 --> 00:44:37.850
And then what it'll do
is it will come back,

00:44:37.850 --> 00:44:42.859
and out of my hotel, it'll
even tell me where I step out--

00:44:42.859 --> 00:44:43.400
where are we?

00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:45.050
It will tell me
the arrival time.

00:44:45.050 --> 00:44:46.850
Like in this case,
when I was calling it,

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:48.564
by the time I arrived
there, it was 3:35.

00:44:48.564 --> 00:44:50.480
So I could check that
against the closing time

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:52.040
to see if I could make it.

00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:55.340
But then it would also
tell me step by step.

00:44:55.340 --> 00:44:57.560
Walk out of my hotel, turn left.

00:44:57.560 --> 00:44:59.330
Walk to Green Park Station.

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:00.360
It's 0.4 of a kilometer.

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:02.180
It will take me six minutes.

00:45:02.180 --> 00:45:03.922
When I'm in Green
Park Station, then it

00:45:03.922 --> 00:45:05.630
will tell me which
Tube lane to get onto.

00:45:05.630 --> 00:45:08.480
The Tube is the London
Underground, the subway.

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:10.130
So it will tell me
which one to get on,

00:45:10.130 --> 00:45:12.200
and it will tell me
where to get off.

00:45:12.200 --> 00:45:14.300
And then once I'm--
so here, I get on it,

00:45:14.300 --> 00:45:17.120
on the subway towards
Walthamstow Central Station,

00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:20.240
which is-- if you know London,
the lines are arranged by color

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:22.410
and name, but also
endpoint of the line.

00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:25.550
Walthamstow Central is the
endpoint of one of the lines.

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:27.680
It will tell me
which one to get on,

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:29.690
it will tell me
which one to get off.

00:45:29.690 --> 00:45:31.430
And then once I'm
off, how far I have

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:33.650
to walk to get to that cafe.

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:36.170
So if I were building a
service like this one,

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:38.480
by tying all of this
type of information

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:42.440
together, by finding
places, by place details,

00:45:42.440 --> 00:45:45.530
by navigation, by routing,
and all of that kind of stuff,

00:45:45.530 --> 00:45:48.140
that one piece of
information, the place ID,

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:50.090
is what allowed me to
tie it all together.

00:45:50.090 --> 00:45:51.860
So I could go, and
I could geek out,

00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:55.050
and I could eat where Sherlock
eats, and that kind of stuff.

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:56.570
So I was trying
to think of a name

00:45:56.570 --> 00:45:58.319
for if I had to build
a service like this,

00:45:58.319 --> 00:46:01.700
and I came up with the name
"Elementary, My Dear Ankur."

00:46:01.700 --> 00:46:02.832
Sorry.

00:46:02.832 --> 00:46:04.415
You said they would
laugh at that one.

00:46:04.415 --> 00:46:04.940
ANKUR KOTWAL: No, I did not.

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:06.064
LAURENCE MORONEY: [LAUGHS].

00:46:06.064 --> 00:46:09.780
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:11.750
So that's, again, if
we think about all

00:46:11.750 --> 00:46:14.030
of these attributes that
are available to you, all

00:46:14.030 --> 00:46:16.670
these geographic attributes,
from latitudes and longitudes

00:46:16.670 --> 00:46:20.150
to addresses to places
to metadata about places,

00:46:20.150 --> 00:46:22.610
that if you use the
intelligence of the place

00:46:22.610 --> 00:46:24.032
ID to bring all
of these together,

00:46:24.032 --> 00:46:25.490
you can walk up
and down the stack,

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:28.940
and you can build scenarios
like I just mentioned there.

00:46:28.940 --> 00:46:32.440
And with that, if you're
watching any of these videos

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:35.140
on YouTube, if you've watched
any of the other sessions,

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:37.540
that's really what we're
trying to strive towards

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:40.030
to make your life a
lot easier in building

00:46:40.030 --> 00:46:43.180
geo-aware applications, so that
you folks will be the ones who

00:46:43.180 --> 00:46:44.800
are building the new scenarios.

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:47.380
Like Ankur mentioned a few
scenarios earlier on that

00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:50.115
weren't-- we didn't even
hear of them 12 months ago.

00:46:50.115 --> 00:46:52.240
And I'm really excited to
see the things that we're

00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:53.980
going to be talking
about in 12 months' time

00:46:53.980 --> 00:46:56.440
that people have done because
they're navigating the stack,

00:46:56.440 --> 00:46:58.690
that they're building
things with this geostack.

00:46:58.690 --> 00:47:00.731
And I would love to hear
what you're going to do.

00:47:00.731 --> 00:47:02.720
And any wrapping
up words from Ankur

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:03.980
before we have any questions?

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:04.080
ANKUR KOTWAL: No.

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:05.360
I was going to open
it up to questions.

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:06.193
Thank you very much.

00:47:06.193 --> 00:47:08.410
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thank you.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:11.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

